  "text": "**Solution 3 is correct: c = 1/22.**\n\nThe problem's hypothesis is that the Wiener norm grows like O(log^{c−ε}|z|) for every ε > 0. Konyagin and Shkredov's quantitative Beurling–Helson theorem (Funct. Anal. Appl., 2015) proves affineness under growth O(log^{1/22−ε}|z|), so c = 1/22. I'm recalling this from memory and did not check the paper.\n\n**Solution 1 (c = 1)** is wrong. Exponent 1 is the conjectured sharp threshold (Kahane's conjecture, with piecewise-linear examples), not a proven result. Its claim that the exponent is 1 is not established.\n\n**Solution 2 (c = 1/2)** is wrong. The claimed \"previously best known exponent 1/22 due to Lebedev\" is inconsistent with the literature as I recall it: 1/22 is the Konyagin–Shkredov exponent itself. The 1/2 value has no support I can identify.\n\nSolution 3 gets the right background, the right attribution, and the right exponent. The claim that Kahane himself proved a √(log log)-type result is less certain, but it does not affect the final answer.\n\nCHOICE: 3",
